<p>This response made me think, thank you for asking it , because at first I didn’t know how I could reply. Thing is, some questions aren’t brought up here to be answered (that’s part of the draw of this website, the questions that aren’t answered), but that last interview is where we see how the anomaly reacts to it all and the story ends without a neat lil bow on purpose.<br />
Part of what makes some SCP stuff so intriguing to me is that not everything is wrapped up all neatly. If something canonically killed 682, it would wrap the story up, but then there’s no more fun to be had making up stories about what can’t cuz now it’s cannon that he’s dead.<br />
To answer more directly, it ends with the anomaly suffering, where we get some answers to some of the questions, and the O5 council decides to study this usually happy creature previously placated by denial in a state of despair as it goes through the cycle of grief. It’s a tragedy, and the real ending is left to the imagination. My answers to the questions? He goes crazy after losing his only friend ever, slipping right into denial where he talks about how it’s his purpose and she’s happy, right? He’d do the same! Overall, I think human nature is to choose happiness every time, as demonstrated by the doctor.</p>

